cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this specification, please cite it as below."
type: software
title: "Agent Context Memory: A Governance-First Specification for AI Agent Context Memory"
abstract: >
  ACM (Agent Context Memory) is a specification for governance-first context memory
  in autonomous AI agent systems. It recognizes that agent memory is fundamentally
  an authorization problem and applies established access control and audit-log
  patterns to this domain. The core contribution is the Mandate Gate: a pre-work
  mandate, confirmed by the principal, must exist in the agent's memory before work
  is a governed interval. ACM defines three memory tiers organized by trust level
  (intent / trace / evidence), structural capture-author separation, a memory kept in
  a repository of its own and bound to repositories of work by identity, a one-rule
  scope layout, collision-safe shared recording with sealed months, and 25 numbered
  requirements with specified checks. ACM is the operationalization of Principles of
  Earned Autonomy (PEA) for the context memory design problem. The specification claims
  no implementation conformance beyond the evidence status stated for each requirement.
